// Domain 1: AI Fundamentals and Literacy (24%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d1-01", d: 1,
  q: "A board member reviews an AI strategy proposal. The proposal uses the terms artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and generative AI (GenAI). The board member asks how the three terms relate to each other.\n\nWhich statement MOST accurately describes the relationship?",
  o: ["GenAI is a subset of ML, and ML is a subset of AI.",
      "ML is a subset of GenAI, and GenAI is a subset of AI.",
      "AI is a subset of ML, and GenAI is a separate field from both.",
      "GenAI and ML are separate subsets of AI that do not overlap."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AI is the broad field of systems that perform tasks that normally require human intelligence. ML is the subset of AI that learns patterns from data. GenAI is built on deep learning, which is a type of ML. Therefore, the three terms are nested.",
      "This statement reverses the relationship between ML and GenAI. GenAI models are trained by using ML techniques. ML also includes many non-generative tasks, such as classification and forecasting. Therefore, ML is the broader category.",
      "AI is the broadest of the three terms, not a subset of ML. GenAI also depends on ML techniques, so it is not a separate field.",
      "GenAI models learn from data by using deep learning, which is an ML technique. Therefore, GenAI is part of ML, not a separate branch that sits beside it."] },

{ id: "d1-02", d: 1,
  q: "A bank has five years of transaction records. Each record is labeled as fraudulent or legitimate. The bank wants a system that flags likely fraud on new transactions as they occur.\n\nWhich type of machine learning (ML) is MOST appropriate for this requirement?",
  o: ["Supervised learning that trains a classification model on the bank's labeled transactions",
      "Unsupervised learning that groups transactions into clusters with similar patterns",
      "Reinforcement learning that rewards the model for each transaction that it blocks",
      "Generative AI that produces synthetic transactions to model normal account behavior"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Supervised learning trains a model on examples that already have the correct label. The bank has historical transactions labeled as fraudulent or legitimate, and it wants to predict one of those two labels for new transactions. Therefore, supervised classification fits the requirement.",
      "Clustering finds natural groupings in data that has no labels. The bank already has labels for the outcome that it wants to predict. Clusters would not directly tell the bank whether a new transaction is fraudulent.",
      "Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards and penalties in an environment. Rewarding the model for blocking transactions would encourage it to block legitimate customers. The bank's problem is a labeled prediction task.",
      "Synthetic data generation can supplement scarce data. However, the bank already has five years of labeled records. Generating synthetic transactions does not produce a model that flags fraud on new transactions."] },

{ id: "d1-03", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer has purchase histories for 2 million customers. The marketing team wants to discover natural customer segments for targeted campaigns. The company has no existing segment labels.\n\nWhich approach will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use unsupervised learning to cluster customers by similar purchase behavior.",
      "Use supervised learning to predict each customer's segment from past labels.",
      "Use reinforcement learning to test campaign offers and learn from responses.",
      "Use a large language model (LLM) to write a profile for each customer."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Clustering is an unsupervised learning technique that groups records by similarity without predefined labels. The retailer wants to discover segments and has no existing labels. Therefore, clustering meets the requirement.",
      "Supervised learning requires labeled examples of the target outcome. The scenario states that no segment labels exist. Therefore, the company has nothing to train a supervised model on.",
      "Reinforcement learning can optimize a sequence of decisions, such as which offer to show. However, the requirement is to discover segments in existing data, not to optimize offers through trial and error.",
      "An LLM can generate text about a customer. However, individual profiles do not identify groups of customers who behave similarly. This approach also adds inference cost for 2 million customers without meeting the requirement."] },

{ id: "d1-04", d: 1,
  q: "A logistics company wants warehouse robots to improve their routes over time. The robots receive a positive signal for fast, collision-free deliveries and a negative signal for delays. No labeled dataset of correct routes exists.\n\nWhich machine learning (ML) approach does this scenario describe?",
  o: ["Supervised learning", "Unsupervised learning", "Reinforcement learning", "Transfer learning"],
  a: [2],
  r: ["Supervised learning needs labeled examples of the correct output. The scenario states that no labeled dataset of correct routes exists.",
      "Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data, such as clusters. The robots learn from reward signals that follow their actions, which unsupervised learning does not use.",
      "Reinforcement learning trains an agent to take actions in an environment to maximize a cumulative reward. The robots receive positive and negative signals based on their actions. Therefore, the scenario describes reinforcement learning.",
      "Transfer learning reuses a model that was trained on one task as the starting point for another task. The scenario does not describe reusing an existing model. The scenario describes learning from rewards."] },

{ id: "d1-05", d: 1,
  q: "A vendor proposes to apply a gradient boosting algorithm to a company's sales history. The vendor says the result will be a forecasting model that the company can use each week. A business analyst must explain the difference between the algorithm and the model to company leaders.\n\nWhich explanation is MOST accurate?",
  o: ["The algorithm is the learning method. The model is the trained result that makes forecasts.",
      "The algorithm is the trained result. The model is the method that learns from the data.",
      "The algorithm is the sales dataset. The model is the software that stores the forecasts.",
      "The algorithm and the model are the same component, described with different names."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An algorithm is the procedure that learns patterns from data. A model is the artifact that the algorithm produces after training. The company uses the model to make weekly forecasts on new data. Therefore, this explanation is accurate.",
      "This explanation reverses the two terms. The trained result that the company uses for forecasts is the model, not the algorithm.",
      "The sales history is the training data, not the algorithm. The model is a learned representation that produces predictions, not a storage system for forecasts.",
      "The terms describe different things. The algorithm is the learning method, and the model is what the method produces from the data. The same algorithm can produce different models from different datasets."] },

{ id: "d1-06", d: 1,
  q: "A company launched a generative AI (GenAI) assistant for its employees six months ago. The company has not re-trained or customized the model since launch. Monthly AI costs have grown each month as more employees use the assistant.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely reason for the cost growth?",
  o: ["Inference costs accrue on every request, so costs rise as usage grows.",
      "Training costs recur monthly for as long as the model stays in production.",
      "The model retrains itself on employee prompts, which adds compute costs.",
      "Model drift increases the compute that each request needs over time."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inference is the use of a trained model to produce an output. GenAI services commonly charge per request or per token for inference. The scenario states that costs grew as more employees used the assistant. Therefore, growing inference volume is the most likely cause.",
      "Training is a one-time or periodic cost that occurs when a model is built or customized. The scenario states that the company has not re-trained or customized the model since launch. Therefore, training costs do not explain the monthly growth.",
      "Foundation models do not update their weights from user prompts during normal use. Updating a model requires a separate training or customization job, which the scenario states did not happen.",
      "Model drift describes declining accuracy when real-world data changes. Drift affects prediction quality, not the compute cost of each request."] },

{ id: "d1-07", d: 1,
  q: "A company's expense policy requires vice president approval for every claim above $5,000. The finance team wants to route claims to the correct approver automatically. A project team proposes a large language model (LLM) to decide the routing.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Configure a workflow rule that routes claims above $5,000 to a vice president.",
      "Fine-tune an LLM on past approvals so that it learns which claims need a vice president.",
      "Prompt an LLM with the policy text, and ask the LLM to choose an approver for each claim.",
      "Train a classification model on past claims to predict the approver for each claim."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Rule-based automation executes fixed logic the same way every time. The policy is a single, explicit threshold. A workflow rule applies the threshold exactly, costs little to run, and is easy to audit. Therefore, AI is not the right solution for this requirement.",
      "Fine-tuning adapts a model's behavior by training on examples. However, the routing logic is already fully defined by one threshold. A fine-tuned model adds training cost and can still route claims incorrectly.",
      "An LLM can interpret policy text. However, LLM outputs are probabilistic, so the model can occasionally route a claim incorrectly. Each request also adds inference cost for a decision that one rule can make exactly.",
      "A classification model learns patterns from historical data. The routing decision does not require learned patterns because the threshold is explicit. The model could also reproduce past routing mistakes."] },

{ id: "d1-08", d: 1,
  q: "A company is reviewing four proposed uses of generative AI (GenAI) in its finance department. The company wants to avoid uses where a probabilistic output creates unacceptable risk.\n\nWhich proposed use is LEAST suitable for GenAI on its own?",
  o: ["Drafting first versions of monthly variance commentary for analysts to edit",
      "Summarizing long vendor contracts for the procurement team to review",
      "Calculating exact payroll tax withholdings that must be auditable",
      "Suggesting categories for uncoded expenses for an accountant to confirm"],
  a: [2],
  r: ["Drafting commentary is a strength of GenAI. Analysts edit the draft before use, so errors are caught. This use is suitable.",
      "Summarization is a common GenAI use case. The procurement team reviews the summary before acting on it. This use is suitable with human review.",
      "Payroll tax calculations must be exact, repeatable, and auditable. GenAI produces probabilistic outputs that can be wrong in plausible-looking ways. A deterministic calculation engine is the appropriate tool. Therefore, this use is the least suitable for GenAI on its own.",
      "Suggesting categories is a classification task with a human confirming each result. Errors are caught before they affect the accounts. This use is suitable with human review."] },

{ id: "d1-09", d: 1,
  q: "A telecom company trained a churn prediction model on customer data from its home market. The model performed well in testing. After the company acquired a competitor in another country, the model performs poorly on the new customers.\n\nWhich data quality issue MOST likely explains the poor performance?",
  o: ["The training data is not representative of the new customer population.",
      "The training data is too old to reflect current home-market behavior.",
      "The training dataset is too small to train an accurate churn model.",
      "The training data is stored in a format that the model cannot read."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Representative data reflects the population that a model will serve. The model learned patterns only from the home market, and customers in the new country can behave differently. Therefore, the model's patterns do not transfer well to the new population.",
      "Timeliness is a data quality dimension. However, the model still performs well on home-market customers. The problem appears only with customers from the new country.",
      "The model performed well in testing, which indicates enough training data for the home market. More home-market data would not teach the model how customers in the new country behave.",
      "A format problem would prevent the model from working at all. The model runs and performs well on home-market customers. Therefore, the issue is what the data represents, not its format."] },

{ id: "d1-10", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer's demand forecasting model was accurate when it launched. Over nine months, customer buying habits shifted toward online orders. Forecast errors have increased steadily. No one has changed the model or its code.\n\nWhich action should the company take to address this issue?",
  o: ["Monitor accuracy against a baseline, and retrain the model on recent data.",
      "Add guardrails that filter the model's forecasts before planners see them.",
      "Replace the model with a rule that applies last year's growth rate to demand.",
      "Restrict access to the model to reduce the risk of prompt manipulation."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Model drift occurs when real-world data or relationships change after a model is trained. Buying habits have shifted, so the patterns that the model learned are outdated. Ongoing monitoring detects the decline, and retraining on recent data restores accuracy.",
      "Guardrails filter harmful or off-topic content from generative AI outputs. The forecasting errors come from outdated patterns, not unsafe content. Filtering forecasts does not make them more accurate.",
      "Last year's growth rate would not reflect the shift toward online orders either. A fixed rule cannot adapt to changing patterns. This approach replaces a fixable problem with a less capable solution.",
      "Access restrictions address security risks such as prompt injection. The scenario describes declining accuracy from changing customer behavior, not misuse of the model."] },

{ id: "d1-11", d: 1,
  q: "A company finds that employees in several teams paste customer data into public AI chatbots to save time. The company has no AI usage policy. Leadership wants to reduce the data exposure risk without losing the productivity benefit.\n\nWhich approach will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Block all AI websites at the network level, and discipline the employees involved.",
      "Classify AI tools as approved, under evaluation, or blocked, and offer a governed tool.",
      "Let each team choose its own AI tools, and ask each team to report its choices yearly.",
      "Send a company-wide reminder that customer data is confidential, and change nothing else."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["A network block stops access to public tools. However, it removes the productivity benefit and gives employees no sanctioned option. Employees often move to personal devices, which increases shadow AI instead of reducing it.",
      "Transparent tool classification tells employees which tools they can use and which data they can put in them. A governed enterprise tool keeps the productivity benefit. Therefore, this approach reduces shadow AI risk and meets both requirements.",
      "Team-by-team decisions create inconsistent standards and no central visibility. Yearly reporting is too infrequent to control ongoing data exposure.",
      "A reminder raises awareness but does not give employees a safe tool or clear rules. Employees would likely continue to use public chatbots, so the data exposure risk remains."] },

{ id: "d1-12", d: 1,
  q: "A company evaluates a foundation model (FM) for several projects. The projects include contract summarization, customer email classification, and a question-answering assistant. A manager asks why one model can support all three projects.\n\nWhich characteristic of FMs BEST explains this?",
  o: ["FMs are pretrained on broad data and can adapt to many different tasks.",
      "FMs are trained separately for each task on a small labeled dataset.",
      "FMs contain a fixed set of rules that cover common business tasks.",
      "FMs retrieve answers from the company's documents for every request."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An FM is a large model that is pretrained on massive, diverse data. The same FM can be prompted or customized for many downstream tasks, such as summarization, classification, and question answering. Therefore, one FM can support all three projects.",
      "Training a separate model on a small labeled dataset for each task describes traditional machine learning. FMs are general-purpose models that are pretrained once on broad data.",
      "FMs learn patterns from data. They do not contain hand-written rules. Rule-based systems also cannot handle open-ended tasks such as summarization.",
      "Retrieving company documents at request time describes retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), which is an optional pattern. RAG is not what makes an FM general-purpose."] },

{ id: "d1-13", d: 1,
  q: "A support team uses a large language model (LLM) to sort incoming tickets into eight fixed categories. The LLM often invents category names that are not on the list. The team needs a fix that it can apply this week with no additional infrastructure.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Revise the prompt to list the eight allowed categories and add a few labeled examples.",
      "Fine-tune the LLM on six months of historical tickets that agents categorized manually.",
      "Switch to an LLM with a larger context window so that the LLM can read longer tickets.",
      "Add a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline that retrieves the team's help articles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Prompt engineering is the fastest and lowest-cost way to steer model output. Listing the allowed categories constrains the output, and few-shot examples show the expected format. This change requires no training and no new infrastructure.",
      "Fine-tuning can improve consistency. However, fine-tuning requires preparing a training dataset and running a training job. This approach does not meet the requirement to apply a fix this week with no additional infrastructure.",
      "A context window sets how much input a model can process at a time. The problem is that the LLM invents categories, not that tickets are too long. A larger context window does not constrain the output.",
      "RAG supplies relevant knowledge to a model at request time. Help articles do not tell the model which eight categories are allowed. RAG also requires new infrastructure."] },

{ id: "d1-14", d: 1,
  q: "A team writes two prompts for the same classification task. Prompt 1 describes the task and asks the model to classify a new review. Prompt 2 describes the task, includes three reviews with their correct labels, and then asks the model to classify a new review.\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the two prompts?",
  o: ["Prompt 1 uses zero-shot prompting, and Prompt 2 uses few-shot prompting.",
      "Prompt 1 uses few-shot prompting, and Prompt 2 uses zero-shot prompting.",
      "Prompt 2 fine-tunes the model, so the model's weights change after each request.",
      "Prompt 2 uses retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to find its three examples."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Zero-shot prompting gives the model a task without examples. Few-shot prompting includes a small number of worked examples in the prompt. Prompt 1 has no examples, and Prompt 2 includes three labeled reviews.",
      "This statement reverses the two terms. Prompt 2 includes examples, which makes it few-shot. Prompt 1 includes no examples, which makes it zero-shot.",
      "Examples in a prompt guide the model only for that request. Few-shot prompting does not change model weights. Fine-tuning is a separate training process.",
      "RAG retrieves relevant content from an external knowledge source at request time. The team wrote the examples directly into Prompt 2, so no retrieval takes place."] },

{ id: "d1-15", d: 1,
  q: "A legal team pastes a 1,500-page set of contracts into a single request to a foundation model (FM). The FM's answers ignore clauses from large parts of the contract set. Shorter contract sets produce complete answers.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The request exceeds the context window, so the FM cannot consider all input.",
      "The temperature setting is too low, so the FM gives overly cautious answers.",
      "The FM was not trained on legal contracts, so it cannot interpret the clauses.",
      "The contracts are unstructured data, which the FM cannot process reliably."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The context window is the maximum amount of input, measured in tokens, that an FM can consider in one request. Shorter contract sets work, but the full set does not. Options include splitting the documents or retrieving only the relevant passages.",
      "Temperature controls how varied an FM's output is. Temperature does not control how much input the FM can consider. Shorter sets work with the same setting, so temperature is not the cause.",
      "If the FM could not interpret legal language, shorter contract sets would fail too. The issue appears only when the input is very large.",
      "FMs are designed to process unstructured text such as contracts. Shorter contract sets produce complete answers, so the data type is not the cause."] },

{ id: "d1-16", d: 1,
  q: "A company reviews its bill for a generative AI (GenAI) service. Requests with long prompts, large amounts of retrieved context, and long responses cost the most. Short requests cost the least.\n\nWhich pricing factor BEST explains the difference in cost?",
  o: ["The number of input and output tokens that each request processes",
      "The number of employees who have accounts in the GenAI service",
      "The number of times the model has been retrained since launch",
      "The number of documents stored in the company's file system"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["On-demand GenAI pricing is commonly based on input tokens and output tokens. Long prompts, retrieved context, and long answers all add tokens. Therefore, token volume explains why some requests cost more.",
      "Per-user pricing is a seat-based model. Seat costs do not vary from one request to the next. The scenario describes cost differences between individual requests.",
      "Retraining is a separate cost that does not depend on the length of individual requests.",
      "Stored documents affect storage costs. Stored documents do not change the cost of a request unless their content is sent to the model as input tokens."] },

{ id: "d1-17", d: 1,
  q: "An HR department wants an assistant that answers employee questions about company policies. The policies change every month. Every answer must cite the policy document that it came from.\n\nWhich approach will meet these requirements with the LEAST ongoing effort?",
  o: ["Use retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to retrieve current policy passages.",
      "Fine-tune a foundation model on the full set of policies after each monthly policy change.",
      "Train a new foundation model on the policies and general HR knowledge once each year.",
      "Rely on a foundation model's general knowledge, and prompt the model to list its sources."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["RAG retrieves relevant passages from a knowledge source and adds them to the prompt at query time. When a policy changes, the company updates the documents, and answers reflect the change immediately. RAG can also return the source document for each answer.",
      "Fine-tuning changes a model's learned behavior. The company would need to repeat training every month to keep up with changes. Fine-tuned models also do not cite the documents that informed an answer.",
      "Training a foundation model is the most expensive customization option. Yearly training would also leave answers out of date for most of the year.",
      "A general model does not know the company's internal policies. Asking for sources can cause the model to invent citations that look plausible."] },

{ id: "d1-18", d: 1,
  q: "An insurer uses a foundation model (FM) to write claim summaries that must follow a specialized structure and tone. The team has tried prompt engineering and few-shot examples, but outputs still vary. The team has 20,000 high-quality example summaries. The underlying knowledge rarely changes.\n\nWhich approach should the insurer take NEXT?",
  o: ["Fine-tune the FM on the example summaries to teach the required structure and tone.",
      "Add retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) that retrieves claim policies for each summary.",
      "Switch to an FM with a larger context window to fit more instructions in each prompt.",
      "Raise the temperature setting so that the FM explores more structures and tones."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Fine-tuning adapts an FM's behavior by training it on labeled examples. The insurer needs consistent structure and tone, has already tried cheaper options, and has many high-quality examples. Therefore, fine-tuning is the appropriate next step.",
      "RAG supplies knowledge at query time. The problem is inconsistent structure and tone, not missing knowledge. The scenario also states that the underlying knowledge rarely changes.",
      "A larger context window allows more input per request. The team has already tried prompts with examples, and outputs still vary. More input space does not change the model's learned behavior.",
      "A higher temperature increases variation in output. The insurer needs more consistent output, so a higher temperature would make the problem worse."] },

{ id: "d1-19", d: 1,
  q: "A company wants to adapt a foundation model (FM) to its business. The options are prompt engineering, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), fine-tuning, and training a new model from scratch. The company wants to start with the option that needs the least data, cost, and expertise.\n\nWhich sequence orders the options from LEAST to MOST resource-intensive?",
  o: ["Prompt engineering, then RAG, then fine-tuning, then training a model from scratch",
      "RAG, then prompt engineering, then training a model from scratch, then fine-tuning",
      "Fine-tuning, then prompt engineering, then RAG, then training a model from scratch",
      "Prompt engineering, then fine-tuning, then training a model from scratch, then RAG"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Prompt engineering needs no training or extra data. RAG needs a knowledge source and a retrieval pipeline. Fine-tuning needs labeled examples and training compute. Training a model from scratch needs the most data, cost, and expertise. AWS guidance recommends starting with the least resource-intensive option.",
      "Prompt engineering needs less effort than RAG because it requires no retrieval pipeline. Training from scratch is also far more resource-intensive than fine-tuning. This order is wrong at both ends.",
      "Fine-tuning requires labeled data and training compute. Prompt engineering and RAG both require fewer resources, so fine-tuning should not come first.",
      "RAG requires far fewer resources than fine-tuning or training from scratch. Placing RAG last reverses its position in the sequence."] },

{ id: "d1-20", d: 1,
  q: "A company's generative AI (GenAI) assistant answers a compliance question. The answer cites a regulation by name and number. The compliance team finds that the regulation does not exist. The answer was fluent and confident.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely reason that the assistant produced this answer?",
  o: ["The model predicts likely word sequences and does not verify facts, so it hallucinated.",
      "The model's context window was too large, so it confused regulations from its input.",
      "The model was fine-tuned on compliance data, which caused it to create new regulations.",
      "The model's temperature was set to zero, which forces it to invent specific details."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Hallucination is output that is fluent but factually wrong. Large language models predict likely sequences of tokens based on learned patterns. They do not check facts against a source. Therefore, a model can produce a confident citation for a regulation that does not exist.",
      "A context window limits how much input a model can consider. A larger window does not cause a model to invent a regulation that was never in its input.",
      "The scenario does not describe fine-tuning. Fine-tuning on accurate compliance data would not by itself cause the model to invent regulations. Hallucination can occur in any generative model.",
      "A temperature of zero makes output more consistent and less random. It does not force a model to invent details. Hallucination can occur at any temperature."] },

{ id: "d1-21", d: 1,
  q: "A company compares two AI solutions. Solution 1 answers employee questions from a knowledge base. Solution 2 receives a goal, breaks it into steps, calls internal systems through APIs, and adjusts its plan based on the results.\n\nWhich statement BEST describes Solution 2?",
  o: ["Solution 2 is an AI agent because it plans steps and uses tools to reach a goal.",
      "Solution 2 is a chatbot with a larger context window than Solution 1.",
      "Solution 2 is rule-based automation because it follows steps in a set order.",
      "Solution 2 is a fine-tuned model because it was trained on the company's internal APIs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AI agents are goal-directed. An agent plans a sequence of steps, calls tools and systems, observes the results, and adapts. Solution 2 has these capabilities. Solution 1 only generates answers, which describes a basic chatbot.",
      "A larger context window lets a model read more input. It does not give a model the ability to plan steps or call systems.",
      "Rule-based automation follows a fixed, predefined sequence. Solution 2 creates its own plan and adjusts the plan based on results, which rule-based automation cannot do.",
      "Tool use does not require fine-tuning. Agents call APIs through defined tool integrations at run time. The scenario describes planning and tool use, not a training process."] },

{ id: "d1-22", d: 1,
  q: "A travel company wants a solution that receives a customer request, checks flight availability, holds a booking, and updates the customer record in its customer relationship management (CRM) system. Company policy requires a human to approve any charge to a customer's card.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["An AI agent with tool integrations for each system and a human approval step before charges",
      "A chatbot that answers questions about flights from a list of frequently asked questions",
      "An AI agent with tool integrations for each system that also charges customer cards automatically",
      "A rule-based script that books the first available flight for every request it receives"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The request requires multiple steps across several systems, which is the agentic pattern. A human approval step before charges meets the policy requirement. Therefore, this solution meets both requirements.",
      "A chatbot that answers frequently asked questions can only answer questions. It cannot check availability, hold bookings, or update records.",
      "An agent with tool integrations can complete the steps. However, charging cards automatically violates the policy that requires human approval for charges.",
      "A fixed script cannot interpret varied customer requests or preferences. Booking the first available flight also ignores what the customer asked for."] },

{ id: "d1-23", d: 1,
  q: "A company inventories its data sources before an AI project. The project will use generative AI (GenAI) to extract insights from content that has no predefined schema.\n\nWhich data sources are unstructured? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Recorded calls from the customer service line",
      "A relational table of orders with fixed columns",
      "Scanned PDF copies of signed supplier contracts",
      "A CSV export of daily prices for each product",
      "A spreadsheet of employee IDs and salary bands"],
  a: [0, 2],
  r: ["Audio recordings have no predefined tabular schema. Therefore, recorded calls are unstructured data.",
      "A relational table organizes data into predefined columns. Therefore, the order table is structured data.",
      "Scanned documents are images of free-form text with no predefined schema. Therefore, scanned contracts are unstructured data.",
      "A CSV file stores values in fixed columns and rows. Therefore, the price export is structured data.",
      "A spreadsheet organizes data into defined rows and columns. Therefore, the employee spreadsheet is structured data."] },

{ id: "d1-24", d: 1,
  q: "A manufacturer photographs every product on its production line. The manufacturer wants to detect scratches and dents automatically before products are packed.\n\nWhich AI capability is MOST appropriate for this requirement?",
  o: ["Computer vision", "Speech recognition", "Time-series forecasting", "Machine translation"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Computer vision analyzes images and video. Defect detection from photos is an image classification or object detection task. Therefore, computer vision meets the requirement.",
      "Speech recognition converts spoken audio into text. The manufacturer's input data is photographs, not audio.",
      "Time-series forecasting predicts future values from historical sequences, such as demand. It does not inspect images for defects.",
      "Machine translation converts text from one language to another. It does not analyze images."] },

{ id: "d1-25", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer has five years of weekly unit sales for each store. The retailer wants to predict next quarter's unit sales for each store. A team member suggests using a generative AI (GenAI) chatbot for the task.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Train a machine learning (ML) time-series forecasting model on the five-year sales history.",
      "Prompt a GenAI chatbot with the sales history, and ask it to estimate the numbers.",
      "Apply a fixed 5% growth rate to last quarter's unit sales for every store.",
      "Use a GenAI image model to generate charts of expected sales for each store."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Numeric prediction from historical structured data is a classic ML forecasting task. A time-series model learns trends and seasonality for each store. GenAI is not automatically the best tool for every AI problem.",
      "A GenAI chatbot generates text by predicting likely tokens. It is not designed for precise numeric forecasting across many stores, and its estimates can be inconsistent.",
      "A fixed growth rate ignores seasonality and differences between stores. This approach discards the patterns in five years of data.",
      "An image model generates pictures. A generated chart is not a forecast because it is not based on a model of the sales history."] },

{ id: "d1-26", d: 1,
  q: "A company is choosing a foundation model (FM) for a customer-facing chat assistant. The assistant will handle about 2 million requests each month. Customers expect replies within two seconds.\n\nWhich factors should weigh MOST heavily in the selection? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Latency and cost per request at the expected volume",
      "Quality on a test set of real customer questions",
      "The total number of parameters in each FM",
      "How recently each FM was released by its provider",
      "The number of other companies that use each FM"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The assistant must reply within two seconds at high volume. Latency determines whether the FM meets the response-time requirement. Cost per request multiplied by 2 million requests drives the business case.",
      "Evaluation on the company's own representative questions shows how well each FM performs on the actual task. Public benchmarks do not always reflect a specific use case.",
      "A larger parameter count does not guarantee better results for a specific task. Larger models are often slower and more expensive, which can conflict with the latency requirement.",
      "A newer FM is not automatically better for a specific use case. The company should evaluate each FM against its own requirements.",
      "Popularity does not show whether an FM meets this company's latency, cost, and quality requirements."] },

{ id: "d1-27", d: 1,
  q: "A company uses the same foundation model (FM) for two tasks. The marketing team wants varied, creative tagline ideas. The compliance team wants consistent, predictable answers to policy questions.\n\nWhich inference setting should the company adjust for each task?",
  o: ["Temperature: higher for the tagline ideas and lower for the policy answers",
      "Temperature: lower for the tagline ideas and higher for the policy answers",
      "Context window: larger for the tagline ideas and smaller for the policy answers",
      "Training epochs: more for the tagline ideas and fewer for the policy answers"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Temperature controls randomness when an FM selects each token. A higher temperature produces more varied output, which suits brainstorming. A lower temperature produces more consistent output, which suits compliance answers. Temperature is set per request with no retraining.",
      "This setting reverses the effect. A lower temperature makes output more predictable, which limits creative variety. A higher temperature makes policy answers less consistent.",
      "The context window is the maximum input size. It does not control how varied or consistent the output is.",
      "Training epochs are part of model training, not inference. Changing them requires a training job, and the company uses the same FM for both tasks."] },

{ id: "d1-28", d: 1,
  q: "An insurer wants AI to assess claims. Each claim includes photos of the damage and a written description from the customer. The insurer wants one model to consider both inputs together.\n\nWhich model characteristic is MOST important?",
  o: ["Multimodal input that accepts both images and text",
      "A large context window that accepts long text inputs",
      "A high temperature setting that produces varied answers",
      "Reinforcement learning from customer satisfaction scores"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multimodal models accept and reason over more than one type of input, such as images and text. Each claim includes both photos and a description. Therefore, multimodal input is the key requirement.",
      "A large context window helps with long text. It does not allow a model to process photos.",
      "Temperature controls output variation. Claim assessment needs consistent output, and temperature does not add image understanding.",
      "Reinforcement learning is a training method. It does not determine whether a model can accept image and text inputs together."] },

{ id: "d1-29", d: 1,
  q: "A company builds a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) assistant over its product manuals. The design converts each manual section and each user question into embeddings.\n\nWhat is the PRIMARY purpose of the embeddings in this design?",
  o: ["To represent text as vectors so the system can find similar meaning",
      "To encrypt the manual sections so that unauthorized users cannot read them",
      "To add the manual content permanently to the foundation model's weights",
      "To compress the manuals so that they take up less space in storage"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An embedding is a numerical vector that represents the meaning of text. Similar meanings produce vectors that are close together. The system compares the question's embedding with the section embeddings to retrieve the most relevant sections.",
      "Encryption protects data from unauthorized access. Embeddings are not an encryption method, and access control is a separate design decision.",
      "RAG supplies content to the model at query time. Embeddings do not change the model's weights. Changing weights requires training or fine-tuning.",
      "Embeddings are used for semantic search, not for storage savings. The original text is still stored so that it can be passed to the model."] },

{ id: "d1-30", d: 1,
  q: "A finance manager asks a generative AI (GenAI) assistant to write a report and gives no other details. The output is generic and unusable. The manager wants to improve the next prompt.\n\nWhich changes will MOST improve the output? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["State the audience, purpose, and key constraints",
      "Specify the format, such as a table of three risks",
      "Repeat the request several times in the same prompt",
      "Shorten the prompt to one word to reduce token costs",
      "Write the entire prompt in capital letters for emphasis"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Context and constraints tell the model who the report is for and what it must cover. Clear instructions are a core prompt engineering principle.",
      "Specifying the output format gives the model a concrete target. Structured requests produce more consistent and usable output.",
      "Repeating the request adds tokens without adding information. It does not tell the model what a good report looks like.",
      "A one-word prompt removes the context that the model needs. The small token saving does not offset an unusable result.",
      "Capital letters do not add information about the task. The model still lacks the audience, purpose, and format."] },

{ id: "d1-31", d: 1,
  q: "A company needs to classify short support messages into five categories. Volume is about 500,000 messages each day, and each result must return in under 300 milliseconds. A large frontier model and a much smaller model both meet the accuracy target on the company's test set.\n\nWhich model should the company choose?",
  o: ["The smaller model, because it meets the accuracy target at lower cost and latency",
      "The larger model, because larger models handle unexpected inputs more reliably",
      "Both models on every message, keeping the answer with the higher confidence score",
      "The larger model for the first year, then the smaller model after a cost review"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The company should choose the simplest, most cost-effective model that meets its success criteria. Both models meet the accuracy target. At 500,000 messages each day with a strict latency limit, the smaller model's lower cost and faster responses are decisive.",
      "Larger models can be more capable in general. However, both models already meet the accuracy target on the company's own test set. The larger model adds cost and latency without a measured benefit.",
      "Running two models on every message doubles inference cost and adds latency. The smaller model alone already meets the accuracy target.",
      "Starting with the larger model incurs higher costs for a year with no measured benefit. The company already has the evidence it needs to choose the smaller model."] },

{ id: "d1-32", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer has 50,000 online product reviews. The retailer wants to measure how customers feel about each product line and track changes each month.\n\nWhich AI capability is MOST directly applicable?",
  o: ["Sentiment analysis of the review text",
      "Object detection on product photos",
      "Anomaly detection on monthly sales data",
      "Reinforcement learning from star ratings"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Sentiment analysis is a natural language processing (NLP) task that classifies the opinion expressed in text. Reviews are unstructured text. Therefore, sentiment analysis directly measures how customers feel.",
      "Object detection identifies items in images. The retailer wants to analyze what customers wrote, not what appears in product photos.",
      "Anomaly detection finds unusual patterns in numeric data, such as a sudden sales drop. Sales data does not show what customers think about each product line.",
      "Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards. Star ratings alone lose the detail in the review text, and the task is analysis, not sequential decision-making."] },

{ id: "d1-33", d: 1,
  q: "A company's business and technical teams describe AI systems in inconsistent terms. The company wants an international standard that provides a common framework and terminology for AI systems that use machine learning (ML).\n\nWhich standard should the company adopt?",
  o: ["ISO/IEC 23053", "ISO/IEC 42001", "ISO/IEC 27001", "ISO 14001"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ISO/IEC 23053 is a framework for AI systems that use ML. It describes the components and functions of ML-based AI systems with shared terminology. The AIB-C01 exam guide names it as an example of a unified AI vocabulary.",
      "ISO/IEC 42001 specifies requirements for an AI management system. It focuses on organizational governance of AI, not on a common framework for describing ML systems.",
      "ISO/IEC 27001 specifies requirements for an information security management system. It does not define AI terminology.",
      "ISO 14001 covers environmental management systems. It does not address AI systems."] },

{ id: "d1-34", d: 1,
  q: "A company classifies AI tools as approved, under evaluation, or blocked, and publishes the list to all employees. A product team asks to use a newly released AI writing tool. The security team has not yet assessed the tool.\n\nHow should the company handle the request?",
  o: ["Classify it as under evaluation, and allow limited use with no sensitive data.",
      "Classify the tool as approved, because the team has a clear business need for it.",
      "Classify the tool as blocked permanently, because no security review exists yet.",
      "Let the product team decide how to use the tool, and ask it to report incidents."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The under evaluation category exists for tools that are pending review. Limited use with no sensitive data lets the team work while the security team assesses the tool. The published status keeps all employees informed, which reduces shadow AI.",
      "A business need does not replace a security assessment. Approving an unassessed tool could expose company data.",
      "A tool that has not been assessed has not failed a review. A permanent block without an assessment removes a legitimate path to new tools and encourages workarounds.",
      "Leaving the decision to one team bypasses the classification system. Reporting incidents after they occur does not prevent data exposure."] },

{ id: "d1-35", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer designs an AI solution for customer requests. One agent receives each request and breaks it into subtasks. The agent delegates the subtasks to specialized agents for billing, shipping, and returns. The first agent then combines their results into one reply.\n\nWhich agent capability does this design demonstrate?",
  o: ["Multi-agent orchestration that uses agent-to-agent communication",
      "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) over billing documents",
      "Fine-tuning of one model for billing, shipping, and returns",
      "Rule-based routing of requests to three human support queues"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In multi-agent orchestration, a supervisor agent breaks down a goal and coordinates specialist agents. The agents pass subtasks and results to each other. The exam guide names agent-to-agent communication and orchestration strategies as core agent capabilities.",
      "RAG retrieves documents to ground a model's answer. The design describes agents that delegate work to other agents, not document retrieval.",
      "Fine-tuning changes one model's behavior through training. The design uses several specialized agents, not a single customized model.",
      "Rule-based routing sends requests to fixed destinations. In this design, an agent plans subtasks and combines results, and specialized agents do the work."] },

{ id: "d1-36", d: 1,
  q: "A lender's model assigns each new loan application a probability of default. The model was trained last year and has not changed since. Each morning, the model scores the previous day's applications.\n\nWhat is the daily scoring step called?",
  o: ["Inference", "Training", "Labeling", "Validation"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inference is the use of a trained model to produce predictions on new data. The model is already trained and now scores new applications each day. Therefore, the daily step is inference.",
      "Training is the process in which a model learns patterns from historical data. The model was trained last year and has not changed, so the daily step is not training.",
      "Labeling assigns the correct outcome to historical examples so that a model can learn from them. The daily step produces predictions for new applications, not labels for training data.",
      "Validation measures a trained model's performance on held-out data before deployment. The daily step scores live applications, which is inference."] },

{ id: "d1-37", d: 1,
  q: "A vendor reports that its churn model is 99% accurate. The vendor measured accuracy on the same records that it used to train the model. The company wants to know how the model will perform on future customers.\n\nWhat should the company request?",
  o: ["Accuracy measured on held-out data that the model did not see during training",
      "A larger training dataset so that the model reaches 100% accuracy on its records",
      "The same accuracy figure measured again on the original training records",
      "A model with more parameters so that it memorizes more customer behavior"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A model can memorize its training records and still perform poorly on new data. Accuracy on held-out data that the model never saw is a fair estimate of how it will perform on future customers.",
      "More training data can help, but it does not answer the question. Accuracy on training records says little about performance on future customers.",
      "Repeating the same measurement on the same records produces the same misleading figure. It does not test the model on unseen data.",
      "A model that memorizes more of its training data can perform worse on new customers. The company needs evidence of performance on unseen data."] },

{ id: "d1-38", d: 1,
  q: "A churn model returns a score of 0.82 for a customer. The retention team asks what the score means and how to act on it.\n\nWhich statement is MOST accurate?",
  o: ["The score is an estimated likelihood, and the business must choose a threshold for action.",
      "The score guarantees that the customer will leave within the next 82 days.",
      "The score means that the model is 82% accurate across all of its customers.",
      "The score means that 82% of the model's training data came from this particular customer."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Many machine learning models output a score that estimates how likely an outcome is. The score is not a certainty. The business decides the threshold at which to act, based on the cost of acting and the cost of missing a customer who leaves.",
      "A model score is a probability-like estimate, not a guarantee. It also does not describe a time period unless the model was built to predict one.",
      "The score describes one customer's estimated likelihood. Overall accuracy is a separate measurement across many predictions.",
      "The score describes the likelihood of an outcome for this customer. It says nothing about how much training data came from the customer."] },

{ id: "d1-39", d: 1,
  q: "A telecom company wants to predict the dollar amount that each business customer will spend next month. The company has three years of monthly billing history.\n\nWhich type of machine learning (ML) task is this?",
  o: ["Regression", "Classification", "Clustering", "Reinforcement learning"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Regression predicts a continuous numeric value, such as a dollar amount. Predicting next month's spending for each customer is a regression task.",
      "Classification predicts a category, such as whether a customer will churn. The company wants a dollar amount, not a category.",
      "Clustering groups similar records without labels. It does not predict a specific value for each customer.",
      "Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards for its actions. The company wants a numeric prediction from historical data."] },

{ id: "d1-40", d: 1,
  q: "A manager reads that deep learning powers most modern speech and image systems. The manager asks how deep learning fits with machine learning (ML).\n\nWhich statement is MOST accurate?",
  o: ["Deep learning is a type of ML that uses neural networks with many layers.",
      "Deep learning is a separate field that replaces ML for all business uses.",
      "Deep learning is a set of hand-written rules for image and speech tasks.",
      "Deep learning is another name for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Deep learning is a subset of ML that uses multi-layer neural networks. It performs especially well on unstructured data such as speech, images, and text, and it underlies generative AI models.",
      "Deep learning does not replace ML, because it is a type of ML. Simpler ML methods remain a better fit for many structured-data problems.",
      "Deep learning models learn patterns from data rather than following hand-written rules.",
      "RAG is a technique that retrieves external content to ground a generative model's answers. It is not another name for deep learning."] },

{ id: "d1-41", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer lists four AI projects for next year. The CIO wants to know which project needs generative AI (GenAI) rather than traditional machine learning (ML).\n\nWhich project requires GenAI?",
  o: ["Writing unique product descriptions for 10,000 new catalog items",
      "Scoring each transaction for the likelihood of payment fraud",
      "Forecasting weekly demand for each product in each store",
      "Predicting which loyalty members are likely to stop buying"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Writing new product descriptions creates new content, which is what GenAI does. Traditional ML predicts values or categories and does not write text.",
      "Fraud scoring is a classification task. Traditional ML models trained on labeled transactions handle it well.",
      "Demand forecasting is a numeric prediction task. Traditional ML time-series models are designed for it.",
      "Churn prediction is a classification task. Traditional ML can predict it from purchase history."] },

{ id: "d1-42", d: 1,
  q: "A bank still uses a credit decision system built in the 1990s. Experts wrote hundreds of if-then rules into the system. The system does not learn from new data.\n\nHow is the system BEST described?",
  o: ["A rule-based AI system that is not machine learning (ML)",
      "An ML system, because it makes credit decisions automatically",
      "A generative AI (GenAI) system, because it produces decisions",
      "A deep learning system, because it contains hundreds of rules"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Expert systems that apply hand-written rules are an early form of AI. They do not learn patterns from data, so they are not ML.",
      "Automation alone does not make a system ML. ML systems learn from data, and this system follows fixed rules that experts wrote.",
      "GenAI creates new content such as text or images. Producing a decision from fixed rules is not generation.",
      "Deep learning uses neural networks that learn from data. The number of rules does not make a rule-based system a deep learning system."] },

{ id: "d1-43", d: 1,
  q: "A manufacturer has 40,000 scanned supplier letters that mention price changes and delivery issues. Its reporting tools can only query database tables. Leaders want to know which suppliers raise the most delivery issues.\n\nWhich approach BEST unlocks this information?",
  o: ["Use AI to extract and classify the issues described in the scanned letters",
      "Add the letters to the existing reporting tools as attachments to each supplier",
      "Build a dashboard over the purchase order table, which already has supplier IDs",
      "Ask suppliers to resend their letters as spreadsheets with fixed columns"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The letters are unstructured data. AI can read the scanned text, extract the issues, and classify them by supplier and type. The results can then be analyzed like structured data.",
      "Attaching files to records does not make their content queryable. The reporting tools still cannot read what the letters say.",
      "The purchase order table does not contain the delivery issues described in the letters. A dashboard over it cannot answer the question.",
      "Asking for 40,000 letters to be resent is impractical, and it ignores the history that already exists."] },

{ id: "d1-44", d: 1,
  q: "A company wants to analyze the topics that customers raise in 100,000 recorded support calls. Its text analytics tool accepts only text.\n\nWhich step must happen FIRST?",
  o: ["Convert the call recordings to text with speech recognition",
      "Translate the call recordings into several additional languages",
      "Delete calls that last longer than ten minutes to reduce volume",
      "Train a new foundation model on the raw audio from the calls"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The recordings are audio, and the analytics tool needs text. Speech recognition converts the audio to transcripts, which the tool can then analyze for topics.",
      "Translation does not turn the recordings into text that the tool can read. The first need is transcription.",
      "Deleting long calls discards data and still leaves audio that the tool cannot read.",
      "Training a new foundation model is costly and unnecessary. Speech recognition services already convert audio to text."] },

{ id: "d1-45", d: 1,
  q: "An insurer predicts claim fraud from structured claim fields. Adjusters also write free-text notes on each claim. A data team proposes adding the notes to the model.\n\nWhich statement about this proposal is MOST accurate?",
  o: ["The notes are unstructured text that can add signal but need text processing first.",
      "The notes add no value, because unstructured data cannot improve predictions.",
      "The notes can replace the structured fields, because free text holds all of the claim details.",
      "The notes can be used as they are, because models read free text like numbers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Free-text notes are unstructured data. They often contain useful signals, such as unusual circumstances. Natural language processing can turn the notes into features that the model can use alongside the structured fields.",
      "Unstructured data can improve predictions when it is processed correctly. Many signals appear only in free text.",
      "Structured fields such as amounts and dates remain important. The notes add to them rather than replace them.",
      "Models need text converted into a form that they can use, such as embeddings or extracted features. Raw notes cannot be fed in as numbers."] },

{ id: "d1-46", d: 1,
  q: "A company trains a model to classify support tickets by urgency. Five teams labeled the training tickets, and each team used its own idea of urgent. The model's urgency predictions are inconsistent.\n\nWhich action will MOST improve the model?",
  o: ["Define shared labeling guidelines, and relabel a reviewed sample.",
      "Train the model for more epochs on the existing set of labeled tickets.",
      "Switch to a larger model that can learn around the inconsistent labels.",
      "Remove the urgency categories, and ask the model to invent its own labels."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A model learns whatever pattern its labels contain, so inconsistent labels teach inconsistent behavior. Shared guidelines and a reviewed relabeling of the data fix the source of the problem.",
      "Longer training on inconsistent labels teaches the model the inconsistency more firmly.",
      "Model size does not fix contradictory labels. A larger model can memorize the contradictions.",
      "Letting the model invent labels removes the business definition of urgency that the company needs."] },

{ id: "d1-47", d: 1,
  q: "A company plans to predict equipment failures from sensor data. A data profile shows that 30% of the sensor readings are missing because of network outages.\n\nWhich data quality dimension is MOST affected?",
  o: ["Completeness", "Accuracy", "Timeliness", "Consistency"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Completeness measures whether all required data is present. Missing 30% of readings is a completeness problem that can hide the patterns that come before a failure.",
      "Accuracy measures whether recorded values are correct. The problem is missing values, not wrong ones.",
      "Timeliness measures whether data arrives when it is needed. The readings are missing entirely, not late.",
      "Consistency measures whether data agrees across systems and formats. The profile shows gaps, not conflicting values."] },

{ id: "d1-48", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer's assistant answers questions from a product knowledge base. Customers complain that it quotes last season's prices. The knowledge base is refreshed once each quarter.\n\nWhich data quality dimension is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["Timeliness", "Completeness", "Representativeness", "Uniqueness"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Timeliness measures whether data is current enough for its use. Prices change more often than the quarterly refresh, so the assistant quotes outdated prices.",
      "Completeness measures whether required data is present. The prices are present but outdated.",
      "Representativeness concerns whether training data reflects the population that a model serves. The issue here is stale content in the knowledge base.",
      "Uniqueness concerns duplicate records. Duplicates would not cause old prices to appear."] },

{ id: "d1-49", d: 1,
  q: "A company combines customer records from three regional systems to train a model. One system stores dates as day/month/year, another as month/day/year, and the third as free text. Many customer ages are calculated incorrectly.\n\nWhich data quality dimension is the problem?",
  o: ["Consistency", "Timeliness", "Completeness", "Relevance"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Consistency measures whether the same data is represented the same way across sources. Different date formats cause the same value to be read differently, which corrupts the calculated ages.",
      "Timeliness concerns whether data is current. The dates are current but formatted differently.",
      "Completeness concerns missing values. The dates are present but interpreted incorrectly.",
      "Relevance concerns whether data fits the use case. Dates of birth are relevant, but their formats conflict."] },

{ id: "d1-50", d: 1,
  q: "A travel company trained a demand model on bookings from two years that included a major travel disruption. Demand during that period was far below normal. The model now underestimates demand.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The historical training period does not reflect current conditions.",
      "The model was trained on too many years of normal booking history.",
      "The booking data was structured, which demand models cannot use well.",
      "The model uses inference, which always lowers the predicted values."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Models learn patterns from the historical data that they are trained on. Data from an unusual period teaches patterns that no longer hold. The company should retrain on data that reflects current conditions, or adjust for the disruption.",
      "The model was trained on the disrupted period, not on too much normal history. More normal history would likely help.",
      "Demand models are designed for structured booking data.",
      "Inference is the use of a trained model. It does not bias predictions downward."] },

{ id: "d1-51", d: 1,
  q: "A garden supply retailer sells most of its products in spring. The retailer wants a demand forecasting model and has eight months of sales history.\n\nWhat is the MAIN limitation of this training data?",
  o: ["It does not cover a full year, so the model cannot learn the seasonal pattern.",
      "It is structured data, so the retailer would need a generative AI model instead.",
      "It is too recent, so the model would need data that is several decades old.",
      "It is too large, so the model would need to be trained on a random sample."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Forecasting models learn seasonality from repeated cycles in historical data. Eight months cannot show even one full yearly cycle. The model cannot reliably learn the spring peak.",
      "Structured sales data is the normal input for forecasting. Generative AI is not required.",
      "Recent data is valuable. The problem is that the history is too short to include full seasonal cycles.",
      "Eight months of sales for one retailer is not too large to use. The limitation is its short coverage."] },

{ id: "d1-52", d: 1,
  q: "A team trains a model on 80% of its historical records. It keeps the other 20% aside and does not use them in training. After training, it measures performance on the 20%.\n\nWhat is the PURPOSE of the 20%?",
  o: ["To estimate how the model will perform on data that it has not seen",
      "To give the model extra examples once the first training run finishes",
      "To store backup copies of the records in case the training data is lost",
      "To reduce training cost by removing a fifth of the records permanently"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Held-out test data shows how well the model generalizes to data that it has not seen. It gives a fair estimate of real-world performance before deployment.",
      "If the model trained on the 20%, those records could no longer give an unbiased measure of performance.",
      "Backups are a data management practice. The 20% is set aside for evaluation.",
      "The records are used for evaluation, not removed. Cost savings are not the purpose."] },

{ id: "d1-53", d: 1,
  q: "A company's customers ask whether the company can show independent certification of its AI management practices. The company is comparing ISO/IEC 42001 and ISO/IEC 23053.\n\nWhich standard should the company pursue certification against?",
  o: ["ISO/IEC 42001, because it sets requirements for an AI management system",
      "ISO/IEC 23053, because it defines requirements for AI management systems",
      "ISO/IEC 23053, because it is the only AI standard that auditors certify",
      "Neither, because no international standard covers AI management"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ISO/IEC 42001 specifies requirements for establishing, operating, and continually improving an AI management system. Organizations can be certified against its requirements.",
      "ISO/IEC 23053 is a framework that describes AI systems that use machine learning. It is a conceptual framework, not a set of management system requirements.",
      "ISO/IEC 23053 provides concepts and terminology. Certification of management practices is done against ISO/IEC 42001.",
      "ISO/IEC 42001 is an international standard for AI management systems."] },

{ id: "d1-54", d: 1,
  q: "A company's legal and data science teams keep misreading AI contracts. The teams use terms such as model, training data, and inference in different ways. Leaders want both teams to share one set of definitions.\n\nWhich approach will BEST resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Adopt terminology from an international AI standard such as ISO/IEC 23053",
      "Let each team keep its own glossary, and translate the terms case by case",
      "Replace all technical terms in contracts with the vendor's product names",
      "Ask the data science team to review every contract before legal signs it"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["International standards such as ISO/IEC 23053 provide a common framework and terminology for machine learning-based AI systems. A shared vocabulary reduces misunderstandings between business, legal, and technical teams. The exam guide lists this as an example of a unified AI vocabulary.",
      "Separate glossaries keep the mismatch in place. Translating each time is slow and error-prone.",
      "Product names change and do not define concepts. They would make contracts less precise.",
      "Extra reviews may catch some errors, but the teams would still use different definitions."] },

{ id: "d1-55", d: 1,
  q: "A vendor states that it holds ISO/IEC 42001 certification. A procurement team asks what the certification tells them about the vendor.\n\nWhich statement is MOST accurate?",
  o: ["The vendor runs an audited management system for developing and using AI responsibly.",
      "Every output from the vendor's AI models is guaranteed to be accurate and free of bias.",
      "The vendor's AI models are certified as secure against every type of cyberattack.",
      "The vendor is exempt from the buyer's own AI risk assessment and vendor review."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ISO/IEC 42001 certification shows that an organization operates an AI management system with policies, roles, risk management, and continual improvement, and that an auditor has assessed it.",
      "Certification covers the management system, not the correctness of every output.",
      "ISO/IEC 42001 is an AI management standard, not a guarantee of security against all attacks.",
      "Certification is useful evidence, but the buyer still needs to assess the vendor for its own use case."] },

{ id: "d1-56", d: 1,
  q: "A payments company uses 400 hand-written rules to flag fraud. Fraudsters change tactics every few weeks, and analysts cannot update the rules quickly enough. The company has millions of labeled transactions.\n\nWhich approach should the company consider?",
  o: ["A machine learning (ML) model that learns fraud patterns from the labeled data",
      "More analysts who write and update the rules faster as tactics change",
      "A single rule that blocks every transaction above the average transaction value",
      "A generative AI (GenAI) model that writes a new rule every morning"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Rules are hard to maintain when patterns are complex and change quickly. An ML model trained on millions of labeled transactions can detect subtle patterns and can be retrained as tactics change.",
      "More analysts add cost and still cannot match how quickly fraud patterns change. The rule-based approach does not scale.",
      "A single threshold would block many legitimate customers and miss fraud below the threshold.",
      "Writing rules daily with GenAI still relies on fixed logic that lags behind new tactics. It also adds risk from unreviewed rules."] },

{ id: "d1-57", d: 1,
  q: "A lender must decline any application from a country under sanctions, with no exceptions. The lender also wants to estimate credit risk for all other applications.\n\nWhich design BEST meets both requirements?",
  o: ["Use a fixed rule for the sanctions check and a model for credit risk",
      "Train one model to learn both the sanctions check and the credit risk",
      "Use hand-written rules for both the sanctions check and credit risk",
      "Use a generative AI (GenAI) chatbot to decide on each application"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The sanctions requirement is fixed and must be exact, so a deterministic rule fits. Credit risk depends on complex patterns, so a model fits. Combining them uses each approach where it is strongest.",
      "A model can make mistakes on a requirement that allows no exceptions. A legal requirement should not depend on learned patterns.",
      "Hand-written rules struggle to capture complex credit risk patterns, which reduces accuracy.",
      "A chatbot produces probabilistic output and is not designed for exact compliance checks or credit scoring."] },

{ id: "d1-58", d: 1,
  q: "Each day, staff copy 500 orders from a web portal into an enterprise resource planning (ERP) system. The fields are identical in both systems, and the steps never change.\n\nWhich solution is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Rule-based automation that copies the fields between the systems",
      "A machine learning model that predicts the value of each field",
      "A generative AI assistant that rewrites each order before entry",
      "An AI agent that decides which orders are worth entering at all"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The task is repetitive, structured, and identical every time. Rule-based automation copies the fields exactly and cheaply. AI adds no value here.",
      "The values already exist in the portal. Predicting them would add errors to a task that only needs copying.",
      "Rewriting orders could introduce errors into data that must be copied exactly.",
      "The task is to enter every order. Letting an agent decide which orders to enter would create gaps."] },

{ id: "d1-59", d: 1,
  q: "A regulator requires a company to prove exactly why each transaction was held for review. The holding criteria are five published conditions.\n\nWhich approach BEST meets this requirement?",
  o: ["Deterministic rules that apply the five published conditions",
      "A deep learning model that learns the conditions from past holds",
      "A generative AI model that writes an explanation after each hold",
      "A clustering model that groups similar transactions for review"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Deterministic rules apply the published conditions exactly and record which one triggered each hold. That gives the regulator a precise, auditable reason for every hold.",
      "A deep learning model might approximate the conditions but cannot guarantee that it applies them exactly, and its reasoning is harder to explain.",
      "An explanation written after the fact may not reflect the actual reason for a hold. It cannot prove exactly why each transaction was held.",
      "Clustering groups similar records but does not apply the published conditions."] },

{ id: "d1-60", d: 1,
  q: "A retailer deploys an AI agent to handle refund requests. The retailer wants fast service for small refunds and wants to limit financial risk from large ones.\n\nWhich autonomy design is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Let the agent refund small amounts, and route larger refunds for approval",
      "Let the agent issue every refund automatically, regardless of the amount",
      "Require human approval for every refund, regardless of the amount",
      "Let customers choose whether the agent or a human processes their refund"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Autonomy should match risk. The agent handles low-value refunds quickly, and human approval covers the large refunds where errors cost the most.",
      "Full autonomy for every amount exposes the retailer to large losses from errors or manipulation.",
      "Approving every refund removes the speed benefit for small refunds, which carry little risk.",
      "Customer choice does not control financial risk, and it creates inconsistent processing."] },

{ id: "d1-61", d: 1,
  q: "An AI assistant answers questions about order totals. It sometimes adds prices incorrectly when it calculates totals in its own text. The company wants exact totals.\n\nWhich capability BEST solves this issue?",
  o: ["Tool use, so the assistant calls the order system or a calculator for totals",
      "A higher temperature, so the assistant tries more ways to add the prices",
      "A longer system prompt that reminds the assistant to add all numbers very carefully",
      "Few-shot examples that show the assistant several correctly added totals"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Language models are not reliable calculators. Tool use lets the assistant call a system that computes exact totals, such as the order system or a calculator, and return the result.",
      "A higher temperature adds randomness, which makes arithmetic errors more likely.",
      "Reminders can help slightly, but the model still predicts digits as text and can still make errors.",
      "Examples improve format and style, but they do not make the model's arithmetic exact."] },

{ id: "d1-62", d: 1,
  q: "A manufacturer wants its purchasing agent to negotiate delivery dates directly with suppliers' AI agents. Leaders are concerned about agents from outside the company.\n\nWhat must the manufacturer establish FIRST?",
  o: ["A standard protocol with agent identity, scoped permissions, and audit logs",
      "A larger foundation model so that its agent wins more of the negotiations",
      "Unrestricted access for supplier agents so that negotiations move quickly",
      "A rule that its agent must accept every date that a supplier agent proposes"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Agent-to-agent communication across companies needs agreed protocols, verified agent identities, permissions limited to the negotiation, and logs for audit. These controls let agents cooperate without exposing internal systems.",
      "Model size does not address trust, identity, or access. The concern is safe interaction with outside agents.",
      "Unrestricted access would expose internal systems to agents that the manufacturer does not control.",
      "Accepting every proposal removes the value of negotiation and could harm the manufacturer's operations."] },

{ id: "d1-63", d: 1,
  q: "A bank automates account opening with AI agents. The steps must always run in the same order: identity check, sanctions screening, then account creation. Auditors must be able to trace every step.\n\nWhich orchestration approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["A predefined workflow that calls agents in a fixed, logged sequence",
      "A fully autonomous agent that plans a new sequence for each customer",
      "A group of agents that negotiate the order of the steps each time",
      "A single chatbot that asks the customer which step to run next"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When steps have a required order and must be auditable, a predefined workflow gives predictable, traceable execution. Agents can still do the work inside each step.",
      "Autonomous planning suits open-ended tasks. It could change the order of required steps, which breaks the requirement and complicates audits.",
      "Negotiated ordering adds unpredictability to a process that must follow a fixed sequence.",
      "Customers cannot decide the order of regulatory checks. The bank must enforce it."] },

{ id: "d1-64", d: 1,
  q: "An HR team wants employees to get answers to policy questions, with a link to the source policy. The assistant does not need to take any actions in HR systems.\n\nWhich solution is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["A retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) assistant over the policy documents",
      "A multi-agent system that can update employee records in HR systems",
      "An autonomous agent that can approve leave requests on the HR team's behalf",
      "A fine-tuned model that is retrained each time any HR policy changes"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The need is to answer questions and cite the source policy. A RAG assistant does exactly that. Agents add complexity and risk that the use case does not require.",
      "Updating records is an action that the scenario does not require. It adds risk with no benefit.",
      "Approving leave is an action with consequences. The team asked only for answers to questions.",
      "Retraining for every policy change is costly and slow, and fine-tuned models do not cite sources."] },

{ id: "d1-65", d: 1,
  q: "A lender monitors its default model by comparing predictions with actual defaults. Actual defaults become known only 12 months after each loan is issued. The lender wants to detect drift sooner.\n\nWhich approach will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Monitor changes in input data and prediction distributions against the baseline",
      "Wait for 12 months of actual defaults before running any monitoring at all",
      "Retrain the model every day on the most recent loan applications that it has received",
      "Switch the model off whenever the number of loan applications changes"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When outcomes arrive late, shifts in the input data and in the distribution of predictions are early warning signs. They can reveal drift months before actual defaults confirm it.",
      "Waiting 12 months leaves the lender exposed to a drifting model for a year.",
      "New applications have no known outcomes yet, so they cannot serve as labeled training data. Daily retraining is not possible without labels.",
      "Application volume changes for many normal reasons. Switching the model off would disrupt lending without detecting drift."] },

{ id: "d1-66", d: 1,
  q: "A company retrains its recommendation model once a year on a fixed date. Last year, accuracy dropped sharply in March and stayed low until the next retraining.\n\nWhich change BEST addresses this issue?",
  o: ["Set performance thresholds that trigger review and retraining when crossed",
      "Move the fixed retraining date from once each year to once each decade",
      "Stop monitoring accuracy between retraining dates to reduce operating cost",
      "Retrain on the same data each year to keep the model's results consistent"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Monitoring against defined thresholds triggers action when performance actually drops, rather than waiting for a calendar date.",
      "Less frequent retraining would leave the model degraded for even longer.",
      "Without monitoring, the company would not know when accuracy drops.",
      "Retraining on old data does not capture the change in behavior that caused the drop."] },

{ id: "d1-67", d: 1,
  q: "A company's generative AI (GenAI) application uses a foundation model from a provider. The provider plans to retire the current model version and move customers to a newer one.\n\nWhat should the company do BEFORE switching versions?",
  o: ["Run its evaluation set on the new version, and compare it with the baseline",
      "Switch immediately, because newer model versions always perform better",
      "Ask users to report any problems that they notice after the switch",
      "Retrain the new version from scratch on the company's historical data"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A new model version can behave differently in quality, tone, safety, or cost. Running the company's own evaluation set before switching shows whether the new version still meets its requirements.",
      "Newer versions are usually more capable in general, but they can still change behavior on a specific task.",
      "Relying on user reports means that problems reach users first. Testing should happen before the switch.",
      "Training a foundation model from scratch is extremely costly and unnecessary. The company needs to evaluate the new version, not rebuild it."] },

{ id: "d1-68", d: 1,
  q: "A data science team monitors a claims triage model. The team asks who should decide the minimum acceptable accuracy that triggers intervention.\n\nWho should set this threshold?",
  o: ["The business owner, with input from data science and risk teams",
      "The model provider, because it built the underlying algorithm",
      "The IT help desk, because it receives complaints about the model",
      "No one, because any accuracy level is acceptable once in production"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Acceptable performance is a business decision that depends on the cost of errors. The business owner sets it, with technical input from data science and risk input from risk teams.",
      "The provider does not know the company's risk tolerance or the cost of errors in its claims process.",
      "The help desk handles support requests. It does not own the business risk of the model.",
      "Without a defined threshold, no one knows when the model needs intervention."] },

{ id: "d1-69", d: 1,
  q: "A company reviews a popular AI note-taking tool. The tool's terms state that it keeps user content and uses it to train its models. The company's policy prohibits sharing confidential data with third parties for training.\n\nHow should the company classify the tool?",
  o: ["Blocked, with the reason published so employees understand the decision",
      "Approved, because the tool is popular and employees already like using it",
      "Under evaluation indefinitely, with no guidance for employees in the meantime",
      "Approved for confidential data only, because that data is the most valuable"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The tool's terms conflict with company policy, so it should be blocked. Publishing the reason helps employees understand the decision and choose approved alternatives, which reduces shadow AI.",
      "Popularity does not override a policy conflict. Approving the tool would expose confidential data.",
      "The review has already found a clear conflict. Leaving the tool in limbo without guidance encourages unsanctioned use.",
      "Confidential data is exactly what the policy protects. This choice is the opposite of what the policy requires."] },

{ id: "d1-70", d: 1,
  q: "An approved AI writing tool updates its terms. The new terms allow the vendor to use customer content to train its models unless customers opt out.\n\nWhat should the company do?",
  o: ["Move the tool back to under evaluation, and review the new terms and opt-out",
      "Keep the tool approved, because it was already reviewed in the past",
      "Block every AI tool that the company uses until each of them has been fully reviewed",
      "Tell employees to stop reading vendor terms, because they change often"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Tool classifications must be reviewed when terms change. Returning the tool to under evaluation pauses risky use while the company checks the new terms and whether an opt-out meets its policy.",
      "An earlier review does not cover new terms. The change could expose company data.",
      "Blocking every tool is an overreaction to one vendor's change and disrupts work across the company.",
      "Terms define how data is used. Ignoring them would hide exactly the risk that the company needs to manage."] },

{ id: "d1-71", d: 1,
  q: "A company is defining criteria to classify AI tools as approved, under evaluation, or blocked.\n\nWhich criteria are MOST important? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["How the tool stores, protects, and uses the data that employees enter",
      "Whether the tool meets the company's security and compliance requirements",
      "How many employees have already installed the tool on their own",
      "Whether the tool has the most attractive interface among its competitors",
      "How often the vendor posts product updates on social media channels"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Data handling determines whether confidential information stays protected. It is a core criterion for any tool that employees will put company data into.",
      "Security and compliance requirements define what the company can accept. A tool that fails them should not be approved.",
      "Existing unsanctioned use shows demand but not whether the tool is safe. Popularity is not a classification criterion.",
      "Interface design affects user experience, not the risk that the tool creates.",
      "Social media activity does not show how the tool protects data or meets requirements."] },

{ id: "d1-72", d: 1,
  q: "A company builds several assistants on the same foundation model (FM). Each assistant must keep a consistent role, tone, and set of rules across every conversation, whatever the user types.\n\nWhere should the company define the role and rules?",
  o: ["In a system prompt that the application sends with every request",
      "In each user's first message, which users write themselves",
      "In the FM's training data, which the company edits each day",
      "In a public webpage that the FM might find on the internet"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A system prompt sets the model's role, tone, and rules for every request, separately from what users type. It keeps behavior consistent across conversations.",
      "Users would write different instructions, so behavior would not be consistent. Users could also omit or change the rules.",
      "The company cannot edit a provider's training data, and retraining is not how role and rules are set for each application.",
      "FMs do not look up webpages to find their instructions. Instructions must be sent with the request."] },

{ id: "d1-73", d: 1,
  q: "A single prompt asks a foundation model (FM) to read a 30-page report, extract the risks, rank them, and draft a memo. The output often skips steps. The team wants more reliable results without training a model.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Break the task into a sequence of smaller prompts, one for each step",
      "Add the phrase 'be thorough' to the end of the existing single prompt",
      "Fine-tune the FM on examples of finished memos from past reports",
      "Increase the temperature so that the FM explores more possible steps"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Complex, multi-step tasks are more reliable when split into a chain of focused prompts. Each prompt handles one step, and its output feeds the next.",
      "A vague instruction adds little structure. The model can still skip steps in a long, complex prompt.",
      "Fine-tuning requires training, which the team wants to avoid. It also may not fix skipped steps.",
      "A higher temperature adds randomness, which makes skipped or inconsistent steps more likely."] },

{ id: "d1-74", d: 1,
  q: "A foundation model (FM) extracts invoice fields for a downstream system. The system requires JSON with specific field names. The FM sometimes returns free text or renames fields.\n\nWhich prompt change will MOST improve the output?",
  o: ["Specify the exact JSON structure, and include a correctly formatted example",
      "Ask the FM to be creative with how it presents the invoice information",
      "Remove all formatting instructions so that the FM chooses its own style",
      "Ask the FM to explain its reasoning in detail before it gives the final answer"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Stating the exact output structure and showing a correct example gives the model a precise target. This is a core prompt engineering technique for structured output.",
      "Creative presentation increases variation, which is the opposite of what the downstream system needs.",
      "Removing instructions leaves the model free to return any format, which causes the problem.",
      "A detailed explanation adds free text that the downstream system cannot parse."] },

{ id: "d1-75", d: 1,
  q: "Twenty analysts each write their own prompts for the same monthly reporting task. Output quality varies widely between analysts.\n\nWhich approach will MOST improve consistency?",
  o: ["Create a tested prompt template for the task, and maintain it centrally",
      "Let each analyst keep refining a personal prompt until results improve",
      "Switch each analyst to a different foundation model to compare results",
      "Limit the task to the analyst whose prompts work best, and remove others"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A shared, tested template gives everyone the best prompt engineering practices. Central maintenance keeps it current, which produces consistent output across analysts.",
      "Individual refinement keeps results inconsistent across analysts.",
      "Different models would add another source of variation.",
      "Removing analysts does not scale and wastes capacity. A shared template spreads the best prompt to everyone."] },

{ id: "d1-76", d: 1,
  q: "A company's assistant writes product comparisons. Longer comparisons stop in the middle of a sentence. Short comparisons are complete. The input is always short.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The maximum output token setting is too low for longer responses.",
      "The context window is too small for the short input that users send.",
      "The model's training data did not include any product comparisons.",
      "The temperature is set too low, which stops responses at random."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A maximum output token limit caps how long a response can be. When a response reaches the limit, it stops, even mid-sentence. Raising the limit, within cost limits, fixes the problem.",
      "The input is always short, so the context window is not the constraint.",
      "If the model lacked knowledge, short comparisons would also suffer. The problem appears only with length.",
      "Temperature controls randomness in word choice. It does not cut responses off."] },

{ id: "d1-77", d: 1,
  q: "In long support chats, a company's assistant begins to ignore instructions and details from the start of the conversation. Short chats work well.\n\nWhich solution BEST addresses this issue?",
  o: ["Summarize or trim earlier turns so key details stay within the context window",
      "Raise the temperature so that the assistant remembers more of the earlier details",
      "Fine-tune the model on transcripts of short support chats that went well",
      "Ask customers to repeat important details in every message they send"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Each turn adds tokens, and long chats can push early content out of the context window or dilute the model's attention to it. Summarizing or trimming older turns keeps the important details within the limit.",
      "Temperature controls randomness. It does not change how much of the conversation the model can consider.",
      "Short chats already work well. Training on them does not solve the limit on long conversations.",
      "Shifting the burden to customers creates a poor experience and does not solve the underlying limit."] },

{ id: "d1-78", d: 1,
  q: "A company sends its entire 300-page policy manual with every question to a foundation model (FM). Answers are good, but costs and response times are high.\n\nWhich change will MOST reduce cost and latency while keeping answer quality?",
  o: ["Retrieve only the relevant sections for each question, and send those",
      "Send the manual twice with every question to improve answer accuracy",
      "Switch to an FM with a much larger context window and a higher price",
      "Remove the manual and rely on the FM's general knowledge of policies"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Token volume drives cost and latency. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) sends only the sections that are relevant to each question, which reduces tokens while keeping answers grounded.",
      "Sending the manual twice doubles the tokens, which increases cost and latency.",
      "A larger context window allows more input but does not reduce the tokens that the company sends. The higher price would raise costs further.",
      "A general model does not know the company's policies, so answer quality would drop."] },

{ id: "d1-79", d: 1,
  q: "A company budgets for a multilingual assistant by assuming one token per English word. The assistant will also serve customers in Japanese and Thai.\n\nWhat should the company do to improve the cost estimate?",
  o: ["Measure token counts on real sample conversations in each language",
      "Assume that every language uses exactly one token for each word",
      "Budget only for English, because tokens cost the same in all languages",
      "Ignore tokens, because multilingual models are priced per customer"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Tokenization differs by language, and some languages use more tokens for the same content. Measuring real samples gives an accurate basis for the estimate.",
      "Token counts per word vary widely by language and model. A single fixed ratio can badly misestimate cost.",
      "The price per token may be the same, but the number of tokens differs by language, so costs differ.",
      "Generative AI services are commonly priced by tokens, whatever languages they serve."] },

{ id: "d1-80", d: 1,
  q: "A law firm wants an assistant that drafts documents in its distinctive house style. The drafts must also reflect the firm's current precedents, which change weekly. The firm is considering fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).\n\nWhich approach BEST meets both requirements?",
  o: ["Fine-tune for the house style, and use RAG for the current precedents",
      "Fine-tune weekly on the precedents, and ignore the house style",
      "Use RAG for the house style, and fine-tune weekly for the precedents",
      "Use prompt engineering only, with no access to the precedents"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Fine-tuning changes a model's learned behavior, which suits a consistent house style. RAG supplies current knowledge at query time, which suits precedents that change weekly. The two approaches combine well.",
      "Weekly fine-tuning is costly and slow for changing knowledge, and this option drops the style requirement.",
      "This option reverses the roles. Style is learned behavior that fine-tuning handles, and changing knowledge is what RAG handles.",
      "Prompting alone may not reach a distinctive style, and without the precedents the drafts would be out of date."] },

{ id: "d1-81", d: 1,
  q: "A pharmaceutical company's foundation model (FM) misunderstands specialized chemistry terms. The team has already added term definitions to prompts, with little improvement. The company has millions of unlabeled internal research documents but few labeled examples.\n\nWhich customization approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Continued pre-training on the unlabeled research documents",
      "Supervised fine-tuning on the few labeled examples available",
      "More term definitions added to every prompt that users send",
      "Model distillation into a much smaller model with fewer parameters"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Continued pre-training trains an FM further on large amounts of unlabeled domain text, so it learns the domain's language. The company has millions of unlabeled documents and has already tried prompting.",
      "Supervised fine-tuning needs many labeled examples, and the company has few. It also targets specific tasks rather than broad domain vocabulary.",
      "The team already tried adding definitions to prompts, with little improvement.",
      "Distillation creates a smaller, cheaper model from a larger one. It does not teach the model the domain's language."] },

{ id: "d1-82", d: 1,
  q: "A retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) assistant gives weak answers. Logs show that the retrieved passages are often unrelated to the question, even though the right documents exist in the knowledge base.\n\nWhere should the team focus FIRST?",
  o: ["Improve retrieval, for example document chunking and metadata",
      "Fine-tune the foundation model on the questions that users asked",
      "Raise the temperature so that answers become more creative",
      "Replace the knowledge base with the model's general knowledge"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The logs show that the problem is retrieval: the right documents exist but are not returned. Improving chunking, metadata, and search quality fixes the root cause.",
      "Fine-tuning changes the model's behavior, but the model is receiving the wrong passages. Better generation cannot fix poor retrieval.",
      "A higher temperature adds randomness. It cannot make unrelated passages relevant.",
      "General knowledge does not include the company's documents, so answers would get worse."] },

{ id: "d1-83", d: 1,
  q: "A company's large foundation model (FM) performs well on one narrow classification task, but inference costs are too high at scale. The company wants a cheaper model with similar quality on this task.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Model distillation, which trains a smaller model from the large model",
      "Continued pre-training of the large model on more general web data",
      "More few-shot examples added to every prompt sent to the large model",
      "A higher temperature setting so that the large model uses fewer tokens"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Distillation uses a large teacher model to train a smaller, cheaper student model for a specific task. The student can keep much of the quality at a lower inference cost.",
      "More pre-training on the large model does not reduce its inference cost.",
      "More examples in each prompt add tokens and increase cost.",
      "Temperature controls randomness, not the number of tokens or the price per token."] },

{ id: "d1-84", d: 1,
  q: "A team plans supervised fine-tuning so that a foundation model (FM) writes claim summaries in a set format. The team asks what data it must prepare.\n\nWhich data does supervised fine-tuning require?",
  o: ["Labeled examples that pair each input with the desired output",
      "Unlabeled text only, with no examples of the desired output",
      "A list of the words that the team wants the FM to avoid",
      "Only the system prompt that the application currently uses"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Supervised fine-tuning learns from examples of inputs paired with the desired outputs, such as a claim file and its ideal summary.",
      "Unlabeled text is used for continued pre-training, not supervised fine-tuning.",
      "A list of banned words can be enforced with filters. It does not teach the model the target format.",
      "A system prompt is used at inference time. Fine-tuning needs training examples."] },

{ id: "d1-85", d: 1,
  q: "A team has two candidate prompts for a customer email assistant. Each team member prefers a different prompt. The team wants an objective way to choose.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Test both prompts on a representative set, and score them on agreed criteria",
      "Choose the prompt written by the most senior member of the team",
      "Choose the longer prompt, because longer prompts always perform better than short ones",
      "Deploy both prompts to all customers, and switch based on complaints"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Prompt changes should be evaluated like any other change. Scoring both prompts on representative examples against agreed criteria shows which one performs better.",
      "Seniority is not evidence of prompt quality.",
      "Longer prompts are not always better. Extra text can add cost and confuse the model.",
      "Testing on live customers exposes them to the weaker prompt, and complaints are a slow, incomplete signal."] },
]);
